In article <1993Oct14.165006.18191@gtc.com>, dav@gtc.com (David L. Markowitz) writes:
>silly@ugcs.caltech.edu ((me)) writes:
>>Next, a suggestion for the dev team (if you're reading...):
>>Make save files portable across big- and little- endian machines.  I play
>>nethack on a cluster with a shared filesystem, and save files aren't 
>>compatible between these machines (presumably because the byte ordering is
>>different).  This could be done by reading and writing integers with your
>>own, fixed functions.  I think it would be a great step forward...
>
>Better yet, use XDR (eXternal Data Representation).  This is part of Sun's 
>ONC/XDR standard.

Don't want to start a flame war but... how many other vendors support 
that "standard"? There exists a good standard portable data representation 
- namely numbers printed out in ASCII. This is probably the easiest way to go 
(make them coded as well, to avoid cheating by simple editing of the
save file - I remember in old Moria your inventory was saved with ASCII
descriptions of items - it was very easy to save the game and 'multiply'
your possesions with a HEX editor ;-)

But: you have to ensure not only that the data is readable across 
platforms, but also that you have essentially the same version of nethack 
on both sides (identical settings of compilation options inclusive). That
might be a bit dificult with the high 'configurability' of nethack (well,
its easy to write the options into save file, read them back and check,
but it might be difficult to ensure that nethack is really identically
configured on your different machines). 
